\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}

Short-form video platforms such as TikTok structure user attention as a rapid sequence of binary micro-decisions: continue watching, or swipe away. Billions of such decisions are made every day, shaping what a population sees, knows, and acts on, though the data behind them rarely leaves the companies that collect it. Platform metrics and much of the surrounding public discourse treat continuous, uninterrupted viewing time as a proxy for user engagement, yet recent behavioral evidence suggests that prolonged, uninterrupted scrolling is at least as consistent with passive, dissociative consumption as with effortful, goal-directed attention. This thesis investigates whether electroencephalography (EEG), recorded unobtrusively during naturalistic short-form video browsing, can discriminate the neural state preceding a voluntary skip decision from the neural state during sustained, non-skip viewing, and whether any such discrimination exceeds what is achieved by the conventional Engagement Index ($\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$), a composite band-power ratio drawn from clinical and educational neuroscience.

Twenty-five participants ($n=25$; 13 female, 12 male; age $M=24.6$, $SD=2.9$) were recorded with a consumer-grade, four-channel Muse S headband (AF7, AF8, TP9, TP10) while freely browsing a personal TikTok ``For You'' feed for 25 minutes in a private, distraction-free setting, following a two-minute resting baseline. Manual swipe events were synchronized to the EEG stream via keypress markers. A literature-motivated labeling scheme operationalized two classes: \texttt{SKIP}, drawn from a 3.0-second imminent-skip period ending 2.0 seconds before each swipe (a gap exceeding both the reported onset of the Bereitschaftspotential and directly measured pre-movement EMG onset in comparable self-paced tasks), and \texttt{STAY}, comprising all remaining continuous, non-boundary viewing. From this labeling, 236 univariate features spanning seven frequency bands, four electrodes, and the raw broadband signal were engineered, including band power statistics, Hjorth parameters, and two novel time-domain frequency descriptors (Peak Frequency, Macro Frequency).

Class separability, quantified via Cohen's $d$, was consistently higher for individual frequency bands, electrodes, and statistical descriptors than for the Engagement Index; the single strongest feature, raw-signal standard deviation at TP10, exceeded the Engagement Index's separability by nearly tenfold. In logistic regression benchmarking against a coin-flip baseline, the Engagement Index achieved above-chance but class-asymmetric performance, detecting \texttt{STAY} states substantially better than \texttt{SKIP} states. Three candidate features identified through the Cohen's $d$ ranking — TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean, and AF8 delta peak frequency — each yielded statistically significant improvements over the Engagement Index in \texttt{SKIP} detection at both the intra-subject and inter-subject level, though absolute performance remained modest and, in the cross-participant setting, did not exceed the chance-equivalent reference. A secondary behavioral analysis further showed that swipes cluster into rapid, individually variable ``burst'' sequences rather than occurring as uniformly spaced discrete events.

These findings indicate that the conventional Engagement Index captures only a limited, asymmetric component of the neural signal associated with short-form video browsing, and that more granular, region- and frequency-specific EEG features carry additional, currently underexploited discriminative information about imminent disengagement. The thesis further contributes a fully reproducible, open-source recording and analysis pipeline for consumer-grade EEG in naturalistic digital-media settings, and discusses the neuroscientific, psychological, behavioral, and public-health implications of these results, while explicitly flagging electromyographic and powerline-noise contamination as unresolved confounds requiring artifact-controlled replication.

\chapter{Introduction}
\label{chap:introduction}

\section{Motivation}

Short-form video platforms have become one of the dominant modes of digital media consumption worldwide, and TikTok in particular has popularized an interaction paradigm in which content is delivered as an effectively infinite, algorithmically curated stream, navigated by a single recurring behavioral unit: the swipe. Each swipe is the outward expression of a micro-decision — to continue watching, or to move on — repeated hundreds of times within a single session. Understanding what happens, neurally and behaviorally, in the seconds preceding this decision is of interest not only to platform design and human--computer interaction research, but increasingly to public health communication, which must compete for exactly this attentional currency when delivering health messages, educational content, or behavior-change interventions through the same channels \cite{mccashin_using_2023}.

The default proxy for engagement used both by platforms and by much of the surrounding research and policy discourse is duration: how long a user continues watching before swiping away. This proxy is intuitive and trivially measurable from platform telemetry, but its behavioral validity is contested. Attention research using cued-recall paradigms has shown that sustained attention is better characterized as a fluctuating, trial-by-trial internal state, decodable from prestimulus EEG activity, than as a fixed property that scales linearly with time-on-task \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}. More directly relevant to the present context, two recent studies interrogating social media use specifically have found that extended, uninterrupted engagement does not reliably track productive attentional states: spontaneous flow experiences are rarely attributed to social media use even during heavy use \cite{knierim_flow_2026}, and extended, uninterrupted scrolling sessions are strongly associated with normative dissociation — users becoming ``lost'' in the feed, unable to later recall what they consumed \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Taken together, this literature suggests that continuous non-skip viewing time is an ambiguous behavioral signal, equally consistent with effortful engagement and with passive, low-encoding consumption.

This ambiguity motivates a different empirical question, one this thesis takes as its central object of study: rather than assuming that duration indexes engagement, can the moment of disengagement itself — the decision to skip — be characterized directly at the level of the EEG signal, independent of any prior assumption about what continuous viewing means? If a decodable neural signature precedes the voluntary skip action, it would offer a behaviorally grounded, physiologically anchored complement to duration-based metrics, and a candidate target for evaluating whether existing composite engagement measures, such as the Engagement Index ($\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$) widely used in educational and human-factors neuroscience \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}, actually capture the process relevant to short-form video interaction. Neuroimaging evidence already indicates that neural signals recorded during video viewing predict individual engagement decisions and aggregate viewing behavior above and beyond self-report and behavioral measures \cite{tong_brain_2020}; the controlled laboratory constraints inherent to fMRI, however, preclude naturalistic paradigms, motivating the consumer-grade EEG approach adopted here at the acknowledged cost of spatial resolution and subcortical access.

\section{Defining Engagement}
\label{sec:intro_engagement_definition}

``Engagement'' is used across public health, media research, human--computer interaction, and neuroscience to mean different things, measured by different instruments, and the term is not interchangeable between them (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definitions}). Because the construct is the object of this thesis, it is stated here, before anything is measured with it.

\begin{center}
\fbox{%
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Working definition (this thesis).} \textit{Engagement is a viewer's effortful, voluntary attention to the content currently on screen, held against the constant availability of the next video, and ending at the swipe.}
\vspace{0.5em}
\end{minipage}}
\end{center}

In the health-communication case that motivates this work, the content on screen is a health message, and the swipe is the moment that message stops being received. Three properties of this definition matter for what follows. It is \emph{effortful and voluntary}, which excludes the passive, dissociative viewing that produces long watch times without recall \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026}. It is defined \emph{against an alternative} --- the next video, always one thumb movement away --- so that holding attention is a real cost rather than the absence of an exit. And it \emph{ends at an observable event}, the swipe, rather than trailing off into a low score, which is what makes it measurable in this paradigm at all: each swipe is a time-stamped moment at which viewing of that content ended, whatever the viewer's state had been before it.

This definition is deliberately narrow. It names a screen and a swipe, and so it holds only where both exist. Whether anything survives when those are removed --- whether the same construct can be stated for a learner, a patient using a digital intervention, or a colleague in distributed work --- is taken up in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated}, once there are results to constrain the answer. Throughout this thesis, \textbf{engagement} is the primary construct label; \emph{attention}, \emph{absorption}, \emph{watch time}, and the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} contrast introduced in Chapter~\ref{chap:methods} name indicators or operationalisations of it, never the construct itself.

\section{Engagement in Health Communication}
\label{sec:intro_health_communication}

This thesis sits in a literature for which engagement is not a secondary outcome but the precondition for any effect at all. Research on digital behaviour change interventions treats an intervention that is not engaged with as one that cannot work, and Yardley et al.\ argue that the target should accordingly be \emph{effective engagement} --- ``sufficient engagement with the intervention to achieve intended outcomes'' --- rather than more engagement \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Perski et al.'s systematic review finds the construct conceptualised in two parts, experiential and behavioural, where the behavioural part is measured as the amount, frequency, duration, and depth of use \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. In practice the behavioural half dominates measurement: Evans et al.\ identify dose and exposure analytics as a principal strength of digital behaviour-change work, while stating that ``what qualifies as a critical threshold for engagement in digital interventions has yet to be established'' \cite{evans_digital_2022}. The field therefore measures engagement largely as quantity of use, and says plainly that it does not yet know what quantity would count. Section~\ref{sec:health_communication_literature} examines this literature and its measurement practice in detail.

Short-form video is an active channel for this work rather than a hypothetical one. Zenone et al.\ set out a public-health research agenda for TikTok specifically \cite{zenone_tiktok_2021}, COVID-19 messaging on the platform has been analysed directly \cite{basch_covid-19_2022}, and McCashin and Murphy's systematic review finds the platform used across a range of public and youth mental-health purposes, with institutional accounts recording markedly lower engagement metrics than individual creators in an accompanying content analysis of 29 accounts \cite{mccashin_using_2023}. Health content is therefore already competing inside the feed and, on the platform's own counters, under-performing --- while those counters remain the duration- and interaction-based measures whose validity the attention literature disputes (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}).

Theories of how a health message persuades once it is received --- narrative transportation \cite{green_role_2000}, entertainment-education \cite{moyer-guse_toward_2008} --- all begin from an audience that is still watching; they explain what a received message does, not whether it is received. This thesis addresses that entry condition with a physiological rather than a usage-based measure --- one instance of the ``complementary and non-intrusive measures of effective engagement'' that Yardley et al.\ name among their priority topics for future research \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.

\section{Research Gap}
\label{sec:intro_research_gap}

Four gaps follow from the literature above and in Chapter~\ref{chap:literature}, and together they produce the research questions in Section~\ref{sec:intro_research_questions}. Each is stated as an open problem the source literature itself identifies, rather than as a claim that nothing has been done.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textbf{G1}] \textbf{Engagement is measured largely as quantity of use, and the field has not established what quantity means.} Amount, frequency, duration, and depth of use form the behavioural half of the reference definition \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017} and the basis of platform and marketing metrics \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. The limits of this are stated within the literature itself: ``more engagement does not necessarily equate to more effective engagement'', and effectiveness ``may or may not require sustained engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}, while the critical threshold ``has yet to be established'' \cite{evans_digital_2022}. Independent evidence points the same way: long uninterrupted viewing is as consistent with dissociative, low-recall consumption as with attention \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026}, attention fluctuates moment to moment rather than accumulating with time \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}, and on a public-health TikTok account video length was negatively associated with both likes and comments \cite{chen_factors_2021}.
% READER-LOAD: "the latter" = Yardley et al., but Perski et al. is the nearer antecedent. Genuinely ambiguous. FIX: name Yardley et al.
    \item[\textbf{G2}] \textbf{Physiological measurement is named as a route to the construct and little used within this literature.} Experience scales, telemetry, band-power indices, and observer ratings are each treated as the construct within their own domain \cite{obrien_what_2008, trunfio_conceptualising_2021, karimah_automatic_2022, bellos_methods_2025}, so a low score cannot distinguish an absent construct from an unsuitable instrument. Perski et al.\ list recording of physiological or psychophysical correlates as one of three routes to measuring engagement, but located such measures only in the computer-science and HCI literatures rather than in behavioural science \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}; neither their inventory nor Yardley et al.'s contains EEG, and validating ``complementary and non-intrusive measures of effective engagement'' is listed among the latter's priority topics for future research \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}.
    \item[\textbf{G3}] \textbf{The standard EEG measure of engagement was developed for a different kind of task.} The Engagement Index \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\) was built to track slow-changing operator vigilance in an automated flight-deck loop \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}, and its classification accuracy has been reported as not optimal in a controlled learning task, where the index is noted to be used mainly in non-predictive applications \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}. Which spectral markers track engagement remains an active question: markers selected for engagement and for sustained attention dissociate within a single naturalistic task \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026}, and inter-subject variability is identified as an open problem across this literature \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. Its behaviour around self-paced decisions on a seconds timescale has not, to our knowledge, been reported.
    \item[\textbf{G4}] \textbf{There is no established method for placing an EEG label around a self-paced action.} Work on window length and label timing assumes an externally cued event with a known onset \cite{moontaha_online_2023}, and where two such parameters have been optimised jointly rather than one at a time, the population-level optimum did not match the individual optimum for many participants \cite{mccartney_optimal_2026}. A swipe has no cue, so the placement of the label relative to the action has to be argued rather than looked up.
\end{enumerate}

% READER-LOAD: refers back to G1-G4 by bare label, and forward to research questions not yet stated. Reader must hold four labels across a section break. FIX: name each gap in one clause as it is invoked.
G1--G3 produce the first two research questions. If quantity of use is not yet a validated measure of the construct, and the physiological route to it is largely unexercised in this literature, then the moment of disengagement is worth characterising directly at the level of the signal, and the field's standard index is worth benchmarking rather than assuming. G4 produces the third: because the labelling scheme required to do this must be argued into existence, its parameters become an object of study rather than a settled configuration.

\section{Research Questions}
\label{sec:intro_research_questions}

This thesis addresses three interrelated research questions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Separability:} Can EEG recorded during naturalistic, self-paced TikTok browsing be used to distinguish the period preceding a voluntary skip decision (\texttt{SKIP}) from continuous, non-skip viewing (\texttt{STAY}), using only a lightweight, consumer-grade, four-channel headband?
    \item \textbf{Benchmark comparison:} How does the class-separation and predictive performance of individual, granular EEG features compare against the conventional Engagement Index, both as a descriptive effect-size measure (Cohen's $d$) and as a predictive model (logistic regression), at both the intra-subject and inter-subject level?
    \item \textbf{Parameter sensitivity:} How sensitive is any observed separability to the specific temporal parameters — window length, pre-swipe safety gap, and imminent-skip period — used to define the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} labeling scheme itself?
\end{enumerate}

Cohen's $d$ measures the difference between two class averages in one feature, using that feature's own standard deviation as the scale. Because both inputs carry the same unit, the unit cancels out.

Answering these questions requires solving a chain of non-trivial methodological problems that do not have an established, off-the-shelf solution in the literature: a naturalistic recording protocol that preserves ecological validity while achieving millisecond-level behavioral synchronization; a principled, literature-motivated labeling scheme for a self-paced (rather than externally cued) behavioral event; a feature space broad enough to allow genuinely comparative evaluation against a composite clinical baseline; and a cross-validation and significance-testing framework robust to the strong temporal autocorrelation and severe class imbalance inherent to naturalistic swipe data. The bulk of this thesis is accordingly devoted not only to answering the three questions above, but to building and documenting a reusable methodological pipeline capable of doing so reproducibly.

\section{Approach}

To address these questions, twenty-eight participants were recruited, of whom twenty-five ($n=25$) were retained for analysis after exclusion of compromised or ineligible recordings (Section~\ref{sec:method_participants}). Each participant was recorded with a consumer-grade Muse S EEG headband while freely browsing their personal TikTok feed for 25 minutes in a private, isolated setting designed to elicit naturalistic viewing behavior, preceded by a two-minute resting baseline used for per-participant signal normalization (Sections~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}--\ref{sec:method_experimental_protocol}). Manual swipe events were synchronized to the continuous EEG stream via a custom keypress-logging architecture built on the Lab Streaming Layer protocol (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}).

% CITE-NEEDED P01: "Because no prior work had established these parameters for a self-p..." | was: precedent | NO CITE IN SENTENCE | no cite present | verify quote against source PDF
A three-parameter labeling scheme was then developed to derive \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} classes from this continuous, self-paced behavioral stream, with default parameter values grounded in the EEG window-length literature and in the Bereitschaftspotential and pre-movement EMG literature governing motor-preparatory contamination near the swipe action itself (Sections~\ref{sec:method_skipstay_labeling_strategy}--\ref{sec:op_stay_skip}). Because, to our knowledge, no prior work had established these parameters for a self-paced, naturalistic swiping paradigm specifically, their influence on downstream class separability was additionally characterized through a one-at-a-time sensitivity analysis (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}) rather than assumed to be optimal by default.

From the resulting labeled dataset, a broad feature space of 236 univariate EEG descriptors was engineered per 1.0-second analysis window, spanning conventional band-power statistics, Hjorth parameters, and two novel time-domain frequency descriptors, alongside features drawn from the undecomposed raw broadband signal (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}). Feature-level and category-level class separability was quantified via Cohen's $d$ and benchmarked directly against the Engagement Index (Section~\ref{sec:method_class_separation_metric}), and the strongest candidate features were subsequently evaluated as standalone logistic regression classifiers against both a chance baseline and the Engagement Index baseline, under both intra-subject (blocked temporal cross-validation) and inter-subject (leave-one-participant-out) evaluation, with Wilcoxon signed-rank testing used to assess statistical significance across the participant cohort (Sections~\ref{sec:method_baselines_and_logistic_regression}--\ref{sec:method_metrics_and_significance}).

\section{Contributions}

This thesis makes six principal contributions, each developed in detail in Chapter~\ref{chap:results}:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item A fully naturalistic, ecologically valid EEG experiment synchronizing consumer-grade EEG with free TikTok browsing behavior in $n=25$ participants, together with a reproducible experimental protocol (Section~\ref{sec:results_exp_learnings}).
    \item An open-source, reusable recording and analysis pipeline covering automated EEG acquisition, behavioral synchronization, artifact-aware preprocessing, feature engineering, and statistically rigorous model evaluation, transferable to related naturalistic EEG paradigms beyond the present study (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}).
