The remainder of this thesis is organized as follows. Chapter~2 (Literature and State of the Art) situates the present work within the literature on attention and engagement in short-form video, sampling-boundary handling and parameter sensitivity in EEG classification, class-separation metrics, time-domain frequency estimation, and EMG and powerline-noise contamination in consumer EEG. Chapter~3 (Materials and Methods) details the participant sample, data acquisition hardware and protocol, labeling strategy and its literature grounding, preprocessing pipeline, feature engineering, and the statistical framework used for model evaluation. Chapter~4 (Results and Discussion) reports the five contributions summarized above and closes with a discussion of their neuroscientific, psychological, behavioral, and applied implications, together with explicitly flagged limitations. Its closing section steps outside the paradigm and states, as a contribution of basic research, an updated definition of the engagement construct that the preceding results constrain, the measurement requirements following from it, and the limits of that definition. The thesis concludes with a set of concrete directions for future research, spanning validation and replication, mechanistic artifact clarification, theoretical development of the engagement construct, methodological expansion, and application to digital public health.


