\section{Adjacent study}
\label{sec:adjacent_studies}
% CITE-NEEDED P02: "One published study sits closest to the present thesis" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{rahman_detection_2025} | quote verified against source PDF
One published study sits closest to the present thesis: Rahman et al.\ set out to detect Short-form Video Addiction (SVA) from wearable sensors, but never measure addiction \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. Let us call it the \emph{adjacent study}. It records EEG with a consumer-grade Muse S headband (the paper names both Gen~1 and Gen~2) at four scalp electrodes, TP9, AF7, AF8 and TP10, sampled at 256~Hz, plus the headband's two amplified auxiliary channels; its band-wise analysis runs on all six channels. A small young-adult convenience sample ($n=10$, ages 20--35) watches short-form video on their own phones, in their own feeds, TikTok included, and may swipe on whenever they please. Let us call that \textit{naturalistic use}, and the feed itself the \emph{infinite feed}.

The adjacent study labels whole blocks of time. A 15-minute block of short-form video viewing is the positive class; a block of ordinary, non-smartphone activity is the negative class, balanced 1:1. Its classifier therefore answers one question: is this person inside the infinite feed, or not? Swipes are incidental to that question. Methodologically, it is a domain-adaptation paper. EEG is paired with electrodermal activity from an Empatica E4 wristband; LSTM encoders map both signals into one shared feature space, a Maximum Mean Discrepancy loss pulls that space together, and an InfoNCE contrastive term aligns the two streams in time. The purpose is a sensor swap: learn with the headband, then deploy with the far less obtrusive wristband alone. Evaluation uses an 80/20 $k$-fold split; no participant-independent split is reported. The model reaches 76.3\% accuracy from EEG alone and 84.2\% with the wristband added.

On frequency, the adjacent study applies a band-stop filter at 50~Hz and its harmonics, although it was likely recorded in the United States, where mains power runs at 60~Hz. It then splits the signal into five bands: Delta 1--4, Theta 4--8, Alpha 7.5--13, Beta 13--30, and Gamma 30--44~Hz. Beta and Gamma are reported as the most informative bands, both above 80\% accuracy. Its spectrum stops at 44~Hz.

\paragraph{Gap the present thesis fills:} The adjacent study asks whether a person is inside the infinite feed; to the author's knowledge, no published work pairs this hardware class with naturalistic use to classify states \emph{inside} the feed at the level of the individual, self-paced swipe. The present thesis does so with two classes that both occur inside the feed: \texttt{STAY}, sustained viewing without a swipe, and \texttt{SKIP}, the seconds just before the swipe. Both are separated by interpretable single-modality EEG features benchmarked against the Engagement Index (Chapter~\ref{chap:results}), so the present thesis' accuracies are not comparable with the adjacent study's as they measure a different thing. The present study also keeps the high-gamma (40-60~Hz) and very-high-gamma (60-100~Hz) bands un-notched, carrying the resulting powerline-noise risk forward as a limitation (Sections~\ref{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma} and~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}).

