\textbf{Zhang et al.} \cite{zhang_eeg-svrec_2024}. \emph{Research goal:} better short-video recommendation from brain signals. 30 participants watch 15-minute sessions of 20--30 videos each: 3,657 viewings of 2,636 videos in total. The platform is real, but the feed is not the participant's own: each participant gets a fresh account, trained during a ten-hour preference week, and the researchers compose every session from three pools: personalised, non-personalised and random. EEG: a 64-channel research cap, 62 EEG channels at 1,000~Hz, recorded continuously during free, self-paced swiping. Labels are self-ratings given after each session --- every video rated on six affective dimensions, on a five-point scale, like a report card written after the lesson --- and the target is recommendation rating prediction. Limits: a 0.5--50~Hz band-pass removes everything above 50~Hz, and the swipe enters only as the share of each video watched, which Zhang et al.\ correlate with EEG video by video. No label sits at the moment of the swipe.

\textbf{Du et al.} \cite{du_understanding_2025, du_eeg_2025}. \emph{Research goal:} show how one-sided (polarised) short-video content shifts viewers' opinion of a figure, and whether EEG reveals the shift. Two papers report one dataset: 24 participants recruited, 23 analysed. A page built by the researchers replaces the platform. In each lab session it serves 40 one-minute clips: 20 about ten historical or fictional figures, 20 film-clip distractors; a ``Next One'' button moves on. Between two lab sessions lies a six-day browsing period in which each figure reaches a participant from one side only, positive or negative, assigned at random per figure --- a news diet with only one side. A classifier then guesses, for each participant--figure pair, which slant was fed. EEG: a 64-channel cap at 1,000~Hz. Input: alpha-band differential entropy at electrode FPz, averaged over time, from four viewings (positive and negative video, before and after the six days). Result: ACC $=0.641$, AUC $=0.672$. Limits: cross-validation is random 5-fold, not split by participant; the skip button unlocks only after 30~seconds; the label is the slant the researchers assigned, only partly a decision of the viewer; and the six-day period at the centre of the design carries no EEG.

\textbf{Zhang and Zhang} \cite{zhang_how_2025-1}. \emph{Research goal:} find which selling approach (transactional or relational) and which displaying style (high or low enthusiasm and humour) of an online celebrity evokes more neural engagement in short-video advertising. 25 participants watch a fixed set of 64 TikTok advertisements of 10--35~s each. EEG: a 64-channel cap at 250~Hz. ``Neural engagement'' here means four EEG indices: emotion, relaxation, attention and cognitive load. The relaxation index is the Engagement Index, $\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$, taken as a negative logarithm. The four indices are compared across conditions by analysis of variance. Limits: no classifier uses them, and each clip plays to the end, so no swipe or skip decision exists. The study shows the Engagement Index in use in short-form-video EEG research.

