\section{Recording Software}
\label{sec:libraries}
One open-source route from a Muse headband to a laptop is \texttt{muselsl}, created by Barachant et al.\ \cite{barachant_muse-lsl_2019}. Picture the \texttt{muselsl data-stream} as the \emph{bridge}. On one side of the bridge sits the Muse headband converting electrode-activity into a Bluetooth signal. On the other side sits the laptop ready to receive the signal. The bridge streams, displays, and records data from InteraXon Muse headsets; it runs as a command-line tool and as an importable Python library, and its current documentation lists the Muse S used in the present thesis as supported.

The bridge feeds the Lab Streaming Layer (LSL), a system built to unify the collection of time-series data in research experiments. LSL puts each data source on the network as a separate stream, gives every stream synchronized timestamps, and gives near-real-time access to every stream; the \texttt{muselsl} documentation calls it standard in EEG-based brain-computer interface research. Picture LSL as one clock on the wall that every stream reads its time from: the \emph{shared clock}.

A second route is Mind Monitor, an app that records the Muse on a phone; the adjacent study used it on Android \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. Both routes ride on Bluetooth, and a Bluetooth connection can drop mid-session.

\paragraph{A contribution of the present thesis:} Every label of the present thesis hangs on one time-stamped swipe, marked by a keypress on the laptop (Section~\ref{sec:method_data_acquisition}), so keypresses and EEG samples must share one clock and every Bluetooth dropout must be accounted for. A custom recording script created by the present thesis' author therefore launches the bridge (\texttt{muselsl stream}), reads the EEG from the shared clock (LSL) with \texttt{pylsl}, flags each \texttt{pynput} keypress onto the first EEG sample within 100~ms of it via a clock offset measured per recording file, and reconnects the headband after a dropout, starting a new file and logging the lost time as a \emph{reconnect gap}. The created work is further described in Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_recording_pipeline}. Also, no analysis window is contaminated by a reconnect gap, and linear interpolation stays inside one recording file, except for the 90-second baseline, which is joined across files should a reconnect fall inside it (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}).

\section{Defining Engagement: Existing Definitions and Their Gaps}
\label{sec:engagement_definitions}

Engagement is the construct the present thesis measures. The fields that measure engagement do not define engagement consistently. O'Brien and Toms found no agreed definition when they set out to deconstruct the term for human--computer interaction \cite{obrien_what_2008}. More than a decade later, a systematic review of social media engagement reached the same verdict. The review describes engagement as polysemic and multidimensional, and finds the behavioural dimension to be the most used proxy for engagement \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. In digital behaviour-change research, Yardley et al.\ call for ``defining engagement more consistently and appropriately'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Definitions exist. Each field has its own definition, and several definitions are tied to the instrument of measurment in the field. Overall, the definitions do not agree.

Five definitions bear on the present thesis. In \textbf{human--computer interaction}, engagement is a quality of user experience, characterised by attributes including challenge, positive affect, endurability, aesthetic appeal, attention, feedback, novelty, interactivity, and perceived control, and unfolding as a four-stage process: point of engagement, sustained engagement, disengagement, and re-engagement \cite{obrien_what_2008}. In \textbf{marketing and platform analytics}, engagement is mostly what the platform counters return: likes, comments, shares, views, and indices derived from these counters \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021}. Short-video advertising research measures engagement as viewer churn, the share of viewers who leave during playback \cite{salminen_engagement_2024}. In \textbf{human-factors neuroscience}, engagement is an index: the ratio \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\), developed to close a biocybernetic adaptive loop in an automated flight-deck task \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}, and later used as the input for classifying mental engagement \cite{nuamah_electroencephalography_2017}. In \textbf{digital health and behaviour-change intervention science}, engagement has two components, experiential and behavioural, where the behavioural component comprises the amount, frequency, duration, and depth of use \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. In \textbf{learning analytics}, engagement is a taxonomy of types. Emotional engagement, measured from affective cues, is the type studied most often, and most datasets rate engagement by external observation, followed by self-report \cite{karimah_automatic_2022}. A recent review of EEG-based engagement research borrows the human--computer interaction definition of O'Brien and Toms word for word \cite{bellos_methods_2025}.

Four gaps follow. The four gaps are the reason the present thesis adopts none of the five definitions as ground truth, to allow for an unbiased exploration of the term.

\paragraph{Gap 1: several definitions are instrument-bound.} In marketing, in human-factors neuroscience, and in learning analytics, engagement is in practice whatever the instrument of the field returns: a counter, an index, or an observer rating. An instrument-bound definition is convenient and circular. A low score cannot tell an absent construct from an unsuitable instrument, so a negative result stays hard to interpret. The literature shows the cost. De Carvalho et al.\ treat the ratio \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\) as a vigilance index, a marker of sustained attention, while human-factors neuroscience treats the same ratio as the Engagement Index \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026, pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. In the same study, markers chosen for engagement failed to separate a sustained-attention test from a cognitive-engagement test, while markers chosen for attention separated the two tests. The authors attribute the failure of the engagement markers partly to a baseline confound and to a small sample ($n=15$) \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026}. In a controlled learning task, the Engagement Index gave classification accuracies that Apicella et al.\ describe as ``not optimal''. Apicella et al.\ note that the Engagement Index is mainly used in non-predictive applications \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}. Findings stated in instrument-bound definitions are hard to accumulate across fields, because no construct-level statement links two such findings to the same construct.

\paragraph{Gap 2: duration does definitional work.} Duration of use is a named component of the behavioural half of the intervention-science definition \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. Duration is also the raw material of platform and marketing metrics such as views and churn (or retention as the inverse of churn) \cite{trunfio_conceptualising_2021, salminen_engagement_2024}. Intervention science itself questions duration. Yardley et al.\ note that ``more engagement does not necessarily equate to more effective engagement'', and that effective engagement ``may or may not require sustained engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. Behavioural evidence points the same way (Section~\ref{sec:engagement_short_form}). Baughan et al.\ found that time spent reading a feed did not significantly predict self-reported dissociation ($p=0.52$) \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Long viewing therefore fits dissociated, low-recall scrolling as well as attentive, ``engaged'' scrolling. Knierim et al.\ found flow attributed to social media in only 2\% of 673 reported flow occurrences \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. DeBettencourt et al.\ show that sustained attention fluctuates from trial to trial \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}. Duration is at best a weak indicator of engagement. Where duration enters a definition, duration cannot be tested against the construct, because duration is partly the construct.

\paragraph{Gap 3: no definition gives a time-resolved account of the exit.} Most definitions describe engagement as a level. Disengagement then becomes a low value instead of an event. The Engagement Index shows the pattern. Pope et al.\ built the Engagement Index to steer an automation loop from a trend in the index over a time interval \cite{pope_biocybernetic_1995-3}. How the Engagement Index behaves around self-paced decisions a few seconds apart has, to the author's knowledge, not been reported. O'Brien and Toms are the exception, because O'Brien and Toms name disengagement as a distinct stage. O'Brien and Toms reconstruct the stages from semi-structured interviews in which participants recount past experiences \cite{obrien_what_2008}. A recalled account cannot date the start of a stage, least of all in states that dampen self-awareness and later recall \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Engagement may end in a single moment, like a door closing (binary). A score is like a dimmer switch (continuous), so a score cannot show the door closing percisely, and a memory of the experience cannot accurately say when the door closed.

\paragraph{Gap 4: person-invariance is assumed rather than shown.} A composite index presumes that one functional form holds across individuals. The evidence suggests otherwise. In an fMRI study of viewers watching a film, Hasson et al.\ found that prefrontal and other higher-order areas did not synchronise across viewers, and link the non-synchronising areas to unique individual variation \cite{hasson_intersubject_2004}. EEG non-stationarity makes cross-subject classification a recognised difficulty even under laboratory control \cite{apicella_eeg-based_2022}, and inter-subject variability remains an open problem across the EEG engagement literature \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. A definition that names a specific indicator inherits the failure of the indicator to generalise, and may mistake a property of the measurement for a property of the construct. In simple terms, some definitions of engagement name one specific signal. Such a definition is like defining fever as 'a high reading on this one thermometer'. If the thermometer reads differently on different people, the definition breaks with it. An error of the thermometer then looks like a feature of fever, which is misleading.

\paragraph{Working definition.}
\label{sec:working_definition}
Taken together, the four gaps call for a definition of engagement that names no instrument and can still be tested. To our knowledge, no existing definition offers both, therefore the present thesis starts from the following working definition:

\begin{center}
\fbox{%
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Working definition (this thesis).} \textit{Engagement is a viewer's effortful, voluntary attention to the content currently on screen, held against the constant availability of the next video, and if present ending latest at the swipe.}
\vspace{0.5em}
\end{minipage}}
\end{center}

The working definition has three properties. First, engagement is \emph{effortful and voluntary}. The first property excludes passive, dissociated viewing, which produces long watch times without recall (Gap~2) \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026}. Second, engagement is held \emph{against an alternative}. The next video is always one thumb movement away, so holding attention carries a real cost. Third, engagement \emph{ends at an observable event}, the swipe. Each swipe is a time-stamped exit (Gap~3), and the swipe needs no particular instrument to be observed (Gap~1). In the health-communication case that motivates the present thesis, the content on screen would be a health message, and the swipe marks the moment the health message definitely stops being received.

The working definition is deliberately narrow for the present thesis use case. The working definition names a screen and a swipe, and so holds only where a screen and a swipe exist. Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated} returns to the definition once the results are concluded, and drops the screen and the swipe terms, to then addresses Gap~4. Throughout the present thesis, \textbf{engagement} is the primary construct label. \emph{Attention}, \emph{absorption}, \emph{watch time}, \emph{sustained viewing}, and the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} contrast name indicators or operationalisations of engagement. The \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} labels of Chapter~\ref{chap:methods} hang on the swipe, and the present thesis declares the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} labels a partly operationalisation of engagement.

\section{Engagement in the Health Communication Literature}
\label{sec:health_communication_literature}

Health communication does not treat engagement as one outcome among several. It treats it as the condition without which an intervention cannot act at all, which makes how the field defines and measures it directly relevant to what this thesis attempts.

The field's reference definition comes from Perski et al.'s critical interpretive synthesis of 117 articles, in which six papers were excluded at full-text screening for referring to engagement without defining it \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. The synthesis yields a two-part construct: engagement with a digital behaviour change intervention is ``(1) the extent (e.g.\ amount, frequency, duration, depth) of usage and (2) a subjective experience characterised by attention, interest and affect'' \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. The same review specifies three routes to measuring it: self-report, automated recording of use, and recording of physiological or psychophysical correlates. The third route is therefore explicitly sanctioned by the field's own framework --- and largely unexercised within it. The physiological and psychophysical measures the review located (cardiac activity, respiratory depth, electrodermal activity, eye tracking) came from the computer-science and HCI literatures, whereas automated tracking of logins, time spent online, and content used was the most common measure in behavioural science \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}. Yardley et al.'s inventory of psychophysiological measures used for task engagement --- skin conductance, heart rate, facial expression, fMRI --- likewise contains no EEG \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. The present thesis operates in precisely that unused third category.

Yardley et al.\ draw the consequence for duration explicitly. Because engagement matters only insofar as it produces the intervention's outcome, they argue the useful target is \emph{effective engagement}, defined empirically as ``sufficient engagement with the intervention to achieve intended outcomes'' --- noting that ``more engagement does not necessarily equate to more effective engagement'', since effectiveness ``may or may not require sustained engagement'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. In this literature duration is therefore not merely a weak indicator of engagement; it is explicitly decoupled from the outcome engagement is measured for.

What is measured instead is dose. Evans et al.\ identify the availability of objective dosage and exposure analytics as a principal strength of digital behaviour-change work, and recommend dose--response designs testing whether more exposure predicts behaviour change \cite{evans_digital_2022}. The same review states that ``what qualifies as a critical threshold for engagement in digital interventions has yet to be established'', and records that post-level impact is in practice computed from a platform formula, impact per post $=$ (Total Engaged Users / Total Reach) $\times$ 100 \cite{evans_digital_2022}. The operative measure is thus a ratio of platform counters whose meaningful threshold is, by the field's own account, unknown.

Three findings indicate what that measure misses on short-form video specifically. First, reach is not delivery: Basch et al.\ analysed 100 videos under \#Coronavirus together with all 17 posts by the World Health Organization in April 2020, totalling 1{,}194{,}081{,}700 views, and found that fewer than 10\% mentioned transmission, symptoms, or prevention --- a proportion that did not improve for the WHO's own videos \cite{basch_covid-19_2022}. Second, the institutional shortfall is a matter of format rather than topic: across the 29 Irish accounts in McCashin and Murphy's content analysis, public-health accounts used humour in 0 of 2 videos and a platform-popular sound in 0 of 2, against 16 of 22 and 17 of 22 for personal creators, and videos using a popular sound averaged 132{,}419 likes against 51{,}161 for those that did not \cite{mccashin_using_2023}. Third, and most directly relevant here, duration works against engagement even by the platform's own counters: Chen et al.'s negative binomial analysis of 355 videos from the Chinese National Health Commission's ``Healthy China'' account, covering January to April 2020, found video length negatively associated with both likes ($\text{IRR}=0.19$, $p<0.001$) and comments ($\text{IRR}=0.39$, $p<0.001$), the one exception being longer videos carrying positively valenced titles, which drew more likes ($\text{IRR}=21.72$, $p=0.04$) \cite{chen_factors_2021}. The same review characterises the wider social-media and youth mental-health literature as ``largely correlational, overly reliant on self-reported and thus unreliable measures, lacking in construct validity'' \cite{mccashin_using_2023}.

The field's stated research agenda does not currently close this gap. Zenone et al., writing when TikTok had passed one billion monthly users and \#MentalHealth alone carried 17 billion tagged views, propose six public-health research priorities for the platform: health-harming product promotion, the quality of medical advice, misinformation, sensitive and hateful content, problematic use, and corporate activity \cite{zenone_tiktok_2021}. Every one of them concerns what is on the platform or how the platform behaves; none concerns whether a message reaching a viewer is actually received by them. The same commentary urges that such research be conducted independently of TikTok \cite{zenone_tiktok_2021}. Both conditions describe the present study, which measures the viewer rather than the content and requires no platform-internal data to do so (Section~\ref{sec:results_pipeline}). Its contribution to this literature is therefore narrow and specific. Yardley et al.\ list the investigation and validation of ``complementary and non-intrusive measures of effective engagement'' among the field's priority topics for future research \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. This thesis supplies such a measure. It uses EEG, which falls under the third of the three routes Perski et al.\ name --- recording a physiological correlate --- the route behavioural science has left underused. It measures the moment a message stops being received, which dose-based measures cannot see at all. Yardley et al.\ also state the caveat that bounds this contribution: differences in attention ``may not always translate into differences in intervention use'' \cite{yardley_understanding_2016}. An EEG measure therefore adds to behavioural endpoints. It does not replace them.

\section{Engagement and Attention in short-form video platforms}
\label{sec:engagement_short_form}
The behavioral status of continuous, uninterrupted viewing on short-form video platforms is contested in the literature. Attention research using cued recall paradigms has shown that sustained attention is a fluctuating internal state — trial-by-trial, moment-to-moment variation in an EEG-decodable prestimulus signal that predicts subsequent encoding — rather than a fixed property that scales with time-on-task \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}. This distinction matters because two recent HCI studies suggest that longer, uninterrupted engagement with social media specifically does not track this kind of productive sustained attention, and may in fact index its absence. In a five-day field study pairing app telemetry with unprompted daily reconstructions, Knierim et al. found that participants spontaneously attributed flow experiences to social media in only 2\% of reported instances, and that within-person increases in social media use — particularly passive, feed-based use — predicted fewer subsequent flow reports and lower momentary readiness \cite{knierim_flow_2026}. Complementarily, Baughan et al. deployed a custom Twitter client and found that extended, uninterrupted scrolling sessions were strongly associated with normative dissociation: users reported becoming "lost" in the feed, losing track of time, and later being unable to recall what they had consumed, with interface features that segmented or interrupted continuous viewing (custom lists, reading-history labels, time-limit prompts) significantly reducing self-reported dissociation \cite{baughan_i_2022}. Taken together, this body of work indicates that duration of continuous, non-skip viewing is an ambiguous behavioral signal: it is equally consistent with effortful, goal-directed engagement and with passive, dissociative consumption in which little of the viewed content is encoded. Our \texttt{STAY} class therefore should not be interpreted as a direct behavioral proxy for sustained cognitive engagement; rather, it captures continuous non-skip viewing time, a necessary but not sufficient behavioral correlate of engagement that the prior literature suggests may just as often reflect disengaged, dissociative viewing.

A further methodological question concerns whether duration-based behavioral proxies for engagement can be validated, or at least cross-checked, against physiological markers, and here the evidence is more cautionary than supportive. Recent work directly contrasting EEG spectral markers of sustained attention against markers of cognitive engagement in a naturalistic learning context found that established engagement-sensitive markers -- notably frontal midline theta -- failed to discriminate between a sustained-attention task and a cognitive-engagement task, whereas posterior beta, occipital alpha, and the classical $\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$ ratio did discriminate reliably \cite{de_carvalho_selection_2026}, though the authors themselves attribute this null result partly to a baseline confound (a post-effort baseline potentially saturated with residual fatigue) and a modest sample (n=15), rather than presenting it as an unqualified failure of frontal midline theta as an engagement marker. The same study further showed that these markers followed distinct, non-linear (U-shaped and inverted-U-shaped) trajectories over the course of a continuous naturalistic task, such that aggregating spectral power over an entire viewing period -- the same logic underlying a duration-based \texttt{STAY} label -- obscures exactly the real-time fluctuations that separate momentary attentional states from one another. Taken together with the behavioral literature reviewed above \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026, debettencourt_sustained_2021}, this indicates that no existing marker, behavioral or neural, has been shown to positively validate continuous non-skip viewing time as a proxy for sustained cognitive engagement; the available evidence instead consistently undermines duration as a proxy, whether via dissociative viewing states, the rarity of flow during passive consumption, the trial-by-trial (rather than duration-locked) nature of attentional fluctuation, or the failure of aggregate engagement markers to track task state. The present thesis therefore does not treat the \texttt{STAY} label as validated evidence of engagement; it instead adopts it as an explicit, literature-motivated behavioral operationalization, and treats the subsequent EEG analysis (Methods Sections~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}--\ref{sec:method_result_synthesis_pipeline}) as an independent empirical test of what, if anything, separates \texttt{STAY} from \texttt{SKIP} at the level of the signal -- rather than as a confirmation of the label's validity.

\section{Parameter Choice in EEG-classification}
Existing evidence on temporal parameter choice in EEG-based classification is concentrated on two variables -- window length and label latency -- and is generally obtained by varying one parameter at a time rather than jointly. Window lengths between 1 and 5 seconds have been tested on both a benchmark dataset (AMIGOS) and wearable-EEG recordings, with a general decline in F1-score as window length increased across affect dimensions, classifiers, and devices, and 1-second windows performing best \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. The effect of label latency has been isolated by artificially delaying the availability of ground-truth labels by the mean stimulus duration (86\,s) while holding the rest of the pipeline fixed: this alone reduced mean arousal F1-score by roughly 20 percentage points (from above 0.82 to 0.637), and left valence classification below the level reported as chance \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. In motor-imagery decoding, time window and frequency bandwidth had previously been optimized individually but never jointly; addressing this with a full grid search across five time windows and 23 frequency bands showed both parameters to significantly affect classification accuracy (repeated-measures ANOVA, $p<0.001$ for each), while their interaction was nominally significant before correction ($p=0.044$) but did not survive the study's Bonferroni threshold, and the population-level optimal combination did not match the individually optimal combination for many participants \cite{mccartney_optimal_2026}. Read together, these studies indicate that temporal parameter choice can substantially affect EEG-classification performance on its own, that label latency in particular can be sufficient to collapse performance toward chance, and that one-at-a-time variation is the prevailing approach for characterizing these effects, notwithstanding its recognized inefficiency relative to designs drawn from statistical experimental theory \cite{saltelli_how_2010}. None of these studies, however, examine parameters governing the temporal placement of a label relative to a naturalistic, self-paced behavioral event rather than an externally imposed experimental cue -- the question addressed by the sensitivity analysis reported below.

