Beyond the window-length and label-latency sweeps already discussed, two further strands of evidence bear on the direction of the effects examined in this thesis. First, on window length: Tzimourta et al.\ segmented EEG epochs into eight discrete lengths from 5\,s to 12\,s for the detection of Alzheimer's Disease and found that classification accuracy increased step-wise with window length across all six classification problems tested, with no reversals \cite{tzimourta_eeg_2019}. This provides independent, monotonic support for the intuition that longer windows -- carrying more samples per estimate -- yield more separable features, in a paradigm unrelated to short-form video but sharing the underlying signal-processing logic. Second, on the placement of a measurement window relative to an upcoming event: Rimbert et al.\ varied the temporal position of the baseline window relative to the next motor-imagery trial and found that baselines positioned closer to the end of the preceding trial (i.e.\ further from the upcoming cue) produced significantly stronger, more spatially global event-related desynchronization and higher classification accuracy than baselines positioned closer to the cue \cite{rimbert_impact_2023}. This is structurally the same question as the gap-length and imminent-skip-period parameters examined here: both ask whether moving a labelled window further from a to-be-predicted motor event improves the separability of that label. Taken together, these two studies motivate the expectation that, in the present paradigm, larger windows and greater temporal distance from the swipe event should both improve class separability. However, this expectation should not be treated as a universal law of EEG parameter selection: Mi et al.\ swept window length for motor-imagery classification and reported the accuracy-optimal window size \emph{per participant} across nine subjects, finding that it differed from subject to subject, with a gap of up to 13\% in accuracy between window choices within a single participant \cite{mi_exploring_2026}. This indicates that window-length and event-distance effects, while directionally supported in the aggregate, can still be idiosyncratic at the individual level. The present sweep is reported at cohort level and was not resolved per participant, so whether the same heterogeneity holds here is untested; a per-participant sweep of the kind Mi et al.\ report is left to future work. The caveat is nonetheless carried forward into the parameter sensitivity analysis in Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}, where gap length, window length, and imminent-skip period are swept one at a time and the resulting trends are reported descriptively rather than optimized into a final parameter choice.

\section{Sampling Boundaries in EEG and Physiological-Signal Classification}
% CITE-NEEDED P06: "For the present work, this prior work motivates treating boundary o..." | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{moontaha_online_2023} | verify quote against source PDF
Boundary handling during epoch labeling is a recurring but often understated methodological choice in EEG- and physiological-signal classification. Rather than allowing a fixed window to span two adjacent labeled events, Moontaha et al.~\cite{moontaha_online_2023} process incoming EEG in non-overlapping tumbling windows and assign a class label to a window only when it falls entirely within a single stimulus interval; windows that straddle the boundary between two labeled segments are excluded from training rather than assigned to either class. This design choice reflects a broader assumption in the field: because short-timescale physiological signals are treated as stationary only within narrow intervals, and because the true onset of a cognitive or behavioral event relative to its recorded label is inherently uncertain, epochs near event boundaries are a primary source of label noise. This is consistent with the label noise literature more broadly: Frénay and Verleysen~\cite{frenay_classification_2014} show that mislabelling is not uniformly distributed across a dataset but is systematically more probable for samples near the classification boundary or in regions where classes are difficult to separate — a noisy-not-at-random (NNAR) pattern rather than a noisy-completely-at-random one. Under this model, label noise concentrated at class boundaries is disproportionately harmful: it not only degrades classification accuracy but increases the number of training samples required to reach a given level of reliability, meaning that a smaller but purer set of boundary-excluded samples can outperform a larger set containing boundary-adjacent noise. Excluding boundary epochs is therefore preferred over resolving the ambiguity through interpolation or majority-vote heuristics, since misassigned boundary epochs directly degrade class separability during training, and the resulting loss in sample count is expected to be offset by the reduction in exactly the type of noise most costly to classifier performance. For the present work, this prior work motivates treating boundary overlap between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} not as an edge case to be smoothed over, but as a structural feature of swipe-event labeling that should be excluded outright, even at the cost of reduced sample count.

% CITE-NEEDED P05: "this prior work motivates excluding boundary-adjacent epochs outright" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{moontaha_online_2023} | verify quote against source PDF
This boundary-handling logic directly informs the present pipeline's windowing procedure. Moontaha et al. process incoming EEG in non-overlapping tumbling windows and assign a class label to a window only when it falls entirely within a single stimulus interval, excluding windows that straddle the boundary between two labeled segments rather than resolving the ambiguity through interpolation or majority-vote heuristics \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. The present pipeline adopts the same principle at the point of window extraction: 1.0-second analysis windows are drawn with no overlap (stride equal to window length) specifically so that no window can span a class boundary, a safety gap, or an adjacent sample, mirroring the tumbling-window exclusion strategy used for stimulus-interval labels. Because short-timescale physiological signals are treated as stationary only within narrow intervals, and because the true onset of a behavioral event relative to its recorded label is inherently uncertain, this prior work motivates excluding boundary-adjacent epochs outright in the present swipe-event paradigm as well, even though the source study used externally-cued stimulus intervals rather than self-paced swipe events.

\section{Sensitivity to Parameter Choice in EEG-based classification pipelines}
% CITE-NEEDED P07: "OAT sweeps of individual temporal parameters are established in EEG..." | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{moontaha_online_2023} | verify quote against source PDF
Methodologically, sensitivity to parameter choice in EEG-based classification pipelines is most commonly assessed through one-factor-at-a-time (OAT) variation, in which a single parameter is varied while all others are held fixed \cite{saltelli_global_2008}. Despite its widespread use, this approach is known to be statistically inefficient \cite{saltelli_how_2010}, and by construction it cannot identify interaction effects between parameters, since no two parameters are ever varied together. Nonetheless, OAT sweeps of individual temporal parameters are established in EEG decoding: window length has been varied in isolation across a 1--5 s range while holding the remainder of the pipeline fixed, showing a general decline in classification performance as window length increases \cite{moontaha_online_2023}, and label latency has been varied in isolation by artificially delaying label availability, which alone reduced performance by roughly 20 percentage points \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. Where the joint effect of two parameters has instead been assessed via full factorial grid search, both parameters were found to significantly affect classification accuracy on their own, but their interaction did not survive correction for multiple comparisons \cite{mccartney_optimal_2026}, illustrating both the value and the limited scope of interaction analysis relative to OAT. No existing study, to our knowledge, applies OAT to parameters governing the temporal placement of a label relative to a self-paced behavioral event, as opposed to an externally cued or delay-based one; the gap length and imminent-skip period examined below extend the same logic of temporal-parameter sensitivity to this self-paced setting.

