Choosing the temporal parameters that define the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} labeling scheme -- window length, gap length, and imminent-skip period -- requires grounding in existing evidence on EEG window sizing and on the timing of pre-movement neural activity, since these two literatures bear directly on two of the three parameters while the third has no comparable prior work. On window length, Moontaha et al.\ swept window sizes from 1 to 5~s on wearable-EEG affect classification and found a general decline in F1-score as window length increased, with 1-second windows performing best across affect dimensions, classifiers, and devices \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. This motivates the present thesis's choice of a 1.0-second default window, rather than a longer window that would risk diluting the rapid, self-paced cognitive transitions this paradigm targets. On gap length, the pre-swipe exclusion window must be wide enough to exclude motor-preparatory activity from the \texttt{SKIP} class: the Bereitschaftspotential, the best-characterized EEG correlate of self-paced voluntary movement, begins as an early, slow-rising negativity approximately 1.5~s before movement onset, with a steeper late component emerging 400--500~ms before onset \cite{lew_detection_2012, shakeel_review_2015}. A 2.0-second gap therefore exceeds the full reported extent of the Bereitschaftspotential with a safety margin, ensuring that windows labeled \texttt{SKIP} reflect pre-decision cognitive state rather than the motor execution of the swipe itself. No comparable literature exists, however, for the imminent-skip period specifically in the context of a self-paced, naturalistic swiping paradigm; unlike window length and gap length, this parameter cannot be anchored to an established finding and is therefore treated in this thesis as a literature-motivated but ultimately heuristic starting value, addressed directly through the one-at-a-time parameter sensitivity analysis in Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity} rather than through a fixed literature-derived justification.

\section{Class Separation Metric}
% CITE-NEEDED P09: "Cohen's \(d\) is established specifically in EEG-based group- and c..." | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{henao_isaza_comprehensive_2026} | verify quote against source PDF
% CITE-NEEDED P10: "This prior work motivates treating Cohen's \(d\), rather than" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{henao_isaza_comprehensive_2026} | verify quote against source PDF
Quantifying how well a candidate label definition or feature separates two behavioral classes requires an effect-size measure that is standardized and scale-invariant, since raw mean differences are not comparable across features expressed in heterogeneous units (band power, frequency counts, raw voltage) or across labeling parameterizations that alter sample size. Cohen's \(d\), the standardized mean difference between two distributions expressed in pooled-standard-deviation units, was developed precisely to meet this requirement: by dividing the raw effect by the common within-population standard deviation, it yields a "pure," dimensionless index that is comparable regardless of the original measurement scale \cite{cohen_statistical_1988}. This property is what makes it suitable here, where the same separability question -- how distinguishable are two classes -- is asked repeatedly across quantities as different as raw broadband voltage, band-relative power, and peak-frequency counts. Cohen's \(d\) is established specifically in EEG-based group- and class-separability analysis: Henao Isaza et al.\ use it to rank and select EEG features by the magnitude of the difference between an at-risk and a healthy control group \cite{henao_isaza_comprehensive_2026}, and Kiarashi et al.\ use it to quantify how strongly a classifier's predicted probabilities separate a clinical from a non-clinical group, explicitly reporting its improvement as a marker of increased class discriminability \cite{kiarashi_sleep-derived_2025}. Both applications mirror the two uses made of the metric in the present work: ranking individual features by their standardized separation between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d}), and tracking how that separation shifts under different labeling parameterizations (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}). This prior work motivates treating Cohen's \(d\) as the primary descriptive currency for separability throughout the labeling-strategy and feature-ranking pipeline.

\section{Peak Frequency as a Time-Domain Frequency Estimator}
