% CITE-NEEDED P15: "A second, independent line of prior work is the zero-crossing rate" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{khambampati_frequency_2023} | verify quote against source PDF
Estimating a signal's frequency content directly in the time domain, without an intervening Fourier transform, is established along two lines. Hjorth's Mobility parameter, one of the earliest and most widely adopted time-domain EEG descriptors, is defined as the square root of the ratio between the variance of a signal's first derivative and the variance of the signal itself, and is explicitly derived to correspond to the standard deviation of the power spectrum -- i.e., a formally grounded mean-frequency estimate obtained without computing that spectrum \cite{hjorth_eeg_1970}. A second, independent line of prior work is the zero-crossing rate, which counts sign changes per unit time and has established use as a time-domain frequency proxy in EEG classification pipelines \cite{khambampati_frequency_2023}. Both methods are validated in the sense that their relationship to the underlying frequency content is derived analytically (Mobility, via spectral moments) or holds exactly for simple periodic signals (zero-crossing rate, for a pure sinusoid). Peak Frequency, as used in the present thesis, follows the same general premise -- that structural features of the raw time-domain signal encode frequency information -- but operationalizes it as a direct count of local maxima per unit time rather than a derivative-variance ratio or a sign-change rate. This specific operationalization has not, to our knowledge, been independently validated against ground-truth frequency in prior work; the closest analogous claim in the literature, that local extrema are informative of a signal's "global frequency" and function as a down-sampled representation of it, is asserted without independent validation in nonlinear time-series analysis \cite{niknazar_new_2018}. Peak Frequency is therefore treated in this thesis as a heuristic, self-defined descriptor motivated by this broader premise rather than as an established measure, and its discriminative value is instead established empirically via the feature-ranking ablation in Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}.

\section{EMG Artifacts in EEG}
Temporal electrode sites are not neutral with respect to artifact risk: they sit directly over the temporalis muscle, one of the two dominant sources of myogenic contamination in scalp EEG, and this contamination is broadband, present even at rest, and not reliably removed by standard correction methods. Goncharova et al.\ characterized the spectral and topographical signature of this contamination directly, showing that temporalis contraction produces EMG with peak power at 40--80~Hz but measurable energy from 0 to over 200~Hz, that this activity is strongest at temporal and adjacent electrodes, and that even weak (15\% of maximum) contraction is sufficient to contaminate frequencies as low as 8~Hz \cite{goncharova_emg_2003}. Because EMG's beta-range peak power has been reported as low as 13--38~Hz at frontal and temporal sites specifically \cite{goncharova_emg_2003}, this contamination risk is not confined to high-gamma or the raw broadband signal alone; it plausibly extends to alpha- and beta-band features at AF7, AF8, TP9, and TP10 as well. Using a fundamentally different validation method---recording scalp EEG before and after complete pharmacological neuromuscular paralysis---Whitham et al.\ confirmed the magnitude of this effect directly rather than inferentially, finding 10- to 200-fold reductions in power above 20~Hz once muscle activity was eliminated, with the largest reductions at electrodes closest to temporal and cranial muscles \cite{whitham_scalp_2007}. Critically, this contamination is not contingent on deliberate muscle activity: using intramuscular single-motor-unit recordings simultaneous with EEG, Yilmaz et al.\ demonstrated that temporalis (and frontalis) motor units discharge tonically even when participants are instructed to rest, that this tonic activity produces detectable interference potentials on nearby EEG electrodes, and that this interference is significantly reduced but never fully eliminated by independent component analysis (ICA), the standard artifact-correction technique \cite{yilmaz_facial_2019}. This has a direct bearing on any interpretation of the broadband, unfiltered EEG signal specifically: because the undecomposed raw signal is not restricted to a conventional frequency band, it is structurally the signal component most exposed to this failure mode, since EMG's broadband character means no low-pass or band-limiting step can cleanly separate it from genuinely neural activity. The present thesis does not apply ICA or other artifact-correction pipelines to the raw broadband signal (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}), and does not deploy dedicated EMG reference channels of the kind used in more recent correction approaches \cite{li_electromyogram_2021}. Consequently, whenever the raw broadband signal or a temporal-site (TP9/TP10) feature emerges as strongly discriminative between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}), this literature motivates treating that result as ambiguous between a genuine neural signature and an artifact of jaw or temporalis muscle tension covarying with the skip decision, rather than treating it as evidence of one or the other by default. This ambiguity is addressed only descriptively in the present work (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}) and is flagged explicitly as a target for future artifact-controlled replication rather than resolved here.

\section{Delta-Band Function in Awake Cognition}
Although delta (0.5--4~Hz) oscillations are most extensively characterized during sleep, a separate and substantial literature establishes that delta oscillatory responses also play a functional role in awake human cognition. Güntekin and Başar's review of evoked and event-related delta responses concludes that delta activity is consistently involved in decision-making and attentional processes across a wide range of cognitive paradigms, and that reduced delta responses during cognitive tasks are a candidate general marker of impaired cognitive processing, observed across Alzheimer's disease, mild cognitive impairment, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and alcoholism \cite{guntekin_review_2016}. From a complementary evolutionary-functional perspective, Knyazev argues that delta oscillations index basic attentional and motivational salience-detection processes that are evolutionarily older than the higher-frequency (theta/alpha/beta) systems typically associated with more advanced, flexible cognitive control; critically, this account predicts that delta-linked processing becomes functionally more prominent precisely when these higher-frequency systems are less dominant or less effective \cite{knyazev_eeg_2012}. Taken together, this literature establishes two points relevant to the present work: first, that delta activity is not merely a sleep or pathology marker but a recognized correlate of decision-making and attention in the awake brain; and second, that delta's relative importance is expected to increase under conditions where composite, higher-frequency-weighted indices of engagement -- such as the classical Engagement Index, $\beta/(\alpha+\theta)$ -- fail to fully capture the relevant cognitive state. Neither line of work, however, has previously been applied to short-form video engagement or to a naturalistic, self-paced swiping paradigm; the only directly comparable evidence comes from short-form-video-addiction (SVA) research and is mixed rather than confirmatory. Xu et al., studying adolescents (mean age 12.3) during passive short-form video viewing, found reduced task-related delta engagement predicted worse working memory and cognitive control, though the effect was small and borderline-significant (F=2.184, p=.040, R\textsuperscript{2}=.115) \cite{xu_neural_2024}. Rahman et al., by contrast, found beta and gamma bands, not delta, to be the strongest predictors, though in a smaller sample (n=10) performing a coarser binary phone-use classification task (not a skip decision), using different gamma-band boundaries (30--44~Hz) than adopted here \cite{rahman_detection_2025}. Neither paradigm resembles the self-paced STAY/SKIP decision studied in this thesis, which is itself evidence that the present question has not previously been addressed rather than that prior evidence on it is merely mixed. This thesis therefore carries forward a specific, literature-motivated but non-committal assumption: that delta activity is a plausible candidate for carrying decision- and attention-relevant variance in the present naturalistic browsing paradigm, motivated by its established general role in awake cognitive attention and salience detection rather than by prior TikTok- or SVA-specific validation, which remains unsettled. Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance} tests this candidacy directly by evaluating delta-band separability against the Engagement Index and against other frequency bands, and Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation} returns to this literature in interpreting the resulting AF8 delta peak-frequency effect.

\subsection{Notch Filtering and the High-Gamma Band}
\label{sec:lit_notch_high_gamma}
Standard practice for removing powerline-noise from EEG is to apply a narrowband notch filter centered on the local powerline-noise frequency (50~Hz in most of Europe). This practice is uncontroversial when the frequency range of interest lies outside the notch, but becomes a substantive methodological choice when it does not: the conventional high-gamma band (40--60~Hz) straddles 50~Hz directly, meaning any notch filter wide enough to reliably suppress powerline-noise necessarily attenuates a portion of the high-gamma spectrum alongside it. This is not merely a hypothetical concern for low-cost, dry-electrode systems specifically. Pattisapu and Ray, comparing a consumer-grade open-hardware amplifier (OpenBCI) against a research-grade system under identical conditions, found that the low-cost device exhibited pronounced powerline-noise contamination together with additional intermodulation artifacts at frequencies flanking the 50~Hz powerline-noise (36~Hz and 64~Hz, symmetric around 50~Hz), an effect attributed to non-linear distortion introduced during amplification and not present in the research-grade comparison system \cite{pattisapu_stimulus-induced_2023}. It is worth noting this risk is not necessarily one-directional: the same comparison also found the consumer device to underestimate true gamma-band amplitude relative to the research-grade reference under matched conditions \cite{pattisapu_stimulus-induced_2023}, indicating hardware-related gamma distortion in this device class is not uniformly an inflationary artifact. Critically, this contamination pattern falls inside the same 40--60~Hz range this thesis treats as high-gamma, meaning that for consumer EEG hardware of the kind used here, the region a notch filter would need to suppress and the region carrying putative cognitive high-gamma signal are not cleanly separable in frequency. No published evidence, to our knowledge, quantifies how much genuine high-frequency cognitive variance a standard notch filter removes as a side effect in this hardware class, nor conversely how much of an un-notched high-gamma signal reflects powerline-noise and intermodulation artifact rather than cortical activity; the literature establishes that the risk exists in both directions without resolving it. Given this, this thesis treats the notched-versus-un-notched decision as an open methodological trade-off rather than a settled default, and elects to preserve the unfiltered signal for all reported analyses so that no frequency content is discarded on an unverified assumption (Section~\ref{sec:method_universal_preprocessing_pipeline}); the resulting risk that reported high-gamma and broadband findings partially reflect powerline-noise contamination is carried forward explicitly as a limitation in the interpretation of those results (Section~\ref{sec:results_interpretation}).

\subsection{Pre-Motor Window and the Limits of the BP Literature}
The choice of a pre-swipe gap wide enough to separate a decision-related signal from the motor act that follows it rests on two distinct bodies of evidence, only one of which speaks directly to motor contamination. The first is the Bereitschaftspotential (BP) literature: the cognitive decision to act voluntarily forms in the cortex significantly before the physical action is executed \cite{libet_time_1983}, manifesting as a slow cortical negativity preceding self-paced movement, with an early component beginning approximately 1.5~s before onset and a steeper late component beginning 400--500~ms before onset \cite{shakeel_review_2015}. This literature is well established but answers a narrower question than it is often taken to answer -- it characterizes when neural \emph{preparation} for movement begins, not when the physical motor act itself, or its peripheral muscular correlate, begins. A gap defined solely by BP onset times therefore establishes that a window lies outside the window of neural movement preparation; it does not by itself establish that the window is free of motor execution or muscular artifact, since the BP and the muscular event it precedes are not the same thing and are not interchangeable justifications for a single design choice. Lew et al.\ close part of this gap directly: in a self-paced reaching paradigm structurally comparable to the present swipe task, arm muscular (EMG) activation was measured, rather than inferred from BP timing, and found to begin on average 263~$\pm$~40~ms before movement onset \cite{lew_detection_2012}. This is the only source in the present review that reports a directly measured, rather than literature-inferred, onset for the peripheral motor event itself. Taken together, these two findings support -- but do not by themselves prove -- the intuition motivating the present design: a gap of several hundred milliseconds to roughly 1.5~s should place a labeled window beyond both the neural preparatory signal (BP) and the measured onset of physical motor execution (EMG) in comparable self-paced tasks. This thesis adopts that intuition as the literature-motivated starting point for the 2.0-second pre-swipe gap (Section~\ref{sec:op_labeling_params}), while treating it explicitly as a margin derived from directly comparable but not identical paradigms -- reaching versus swiping, arm versus thumb -- rather than as a guarantee that the resulting \texttt{SKIP} window is free of motor-related signal. The task-specific validity of this assumption is not established by prior literature and is not tested independently in the present work; it is a boundary condition carried forward into the parameter sensitivity analysis (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}) rather than a settled fact.



