\section{Data Acquisition}
\label{sec:method_data_acquisition}
Electroencephalography (EEG) data were recorded using a consumer-grade Muse S headband (Generation 2, see Figure \ref{fig:muse_headband}), which has been used successfully in similar EEG-based tasks \cite{krigolson_choosing_2017, apicella_eeg-based_2022, moontaha_online_2023} and has seen broader adoption in peer-reviewed EEG-based user engagement research \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. The device features four dry electrodes placed at positions AF7, AF8, TP9, and TP10 according to the international 10--20 system, with the reference electrode located at Fpz. Validation work indicates that this system yields statistically quantifiable ERP components (N200, P300, reward positivity) at exactly these electrode sites, indistinguishable from those recorded with a research-grade 64-channel system, though this validation is bounded to low-frequency ($<$15~Hz) ERP components \cite{krigolson_choosing_2017}. Two physical units of the same model were used across the cohort; device-related effects therefore cannot be fully separated from participant-related effects (see Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}). Data were continuously sampled at a rate of 256~Hz, consistent with the hardware specifications of the Muse S headband \cite{rahman_detection_2025}.

