\subsection{Baseline Exclusion}
The manually categorized pre-task resting baseline phases were excluded from the \texttt{STAY}/\texttt{SKIP} classification dataset and used only for per-participant signal normalization. Consequently, the algorithms were trained to discriminate between continuous non-skip viewing (\texttt{STAY}) and the period preceding a swipe-away event (\texttt{SKIP}) during browsing, rather than between resting and viewing. Figure~\ref{fig:labeling_strategy} illustrates the default labeling logic, including the press markers and the temporal placement of the labeling windows.

In addition to baseline exclusion, the labeling procedure omitted segments affected by boundary overlap between \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} definitions to preserve clear class assignment and avoid ambiguous temporal regions near each swipe event, following established practice of excluding windows that do not lie entirely within a single labeled interval \cite{moontaha_online_2023}. Boundary-adjacent mislabelling is known to be more probable and more consequential than uniformly distributed label noise \cite{frenay_classification_2014}, motivating this exclusion despite the resulting reduction in sample count. The final label construction therefore prioritized temporal purity of the target classes over maximal sample count.

\subsection{Parameter Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}

To assess how label definition affects class separability, a controlled one-at-a-time (OAT) sensitivity analysis \cite[\S2.4.2]{saltelli_global_2008} over three temporal parameters was conducted: in each experiment two parameters were held constant while the third was varied, so that any change in separability is attributable to the varied parameter alone. This follows established practice in EEG decoding, where window length \cite{moontaha_online_2023} and label latency \cite{moontaha_online_2023} have been varied in isolation to quantify their effect on classification performance. Two limitations follow from the design. First, attribution is local to the operating point at which the remaining parameters were fixed \cite{saltelli_global_2008}. Second, interaction effects are not identifiable under an OAT scheme by construction; resolving them would require a factorial sweep of the kind used for time window and frequency band in motor-imagery decoding \cite{mccartney_optimal_2026}. Independent evidence for monotonic window-length gains comes from Tzimourta et al., who found EEG-classification accuracy increased step-wise from 5\,s to 12\,s windows across six classification problems with no exceptions \cite{tzimourta_eeg_2019}. Similarly, in MI-BCI paradigms, baselines positioned further from the upcoming task cue yielded significantly stronger ERD and higher classification accuracy than baselines positioned closer to the cue \cite{rimbert_impact_2023}, mirroring the present gap-length and imminent-skip-period sensitivity. However, window-length effects are not always monotonic: a multi-scale CSP study found subject-specific optimal window sizes with accuracy differences of up to 13\% depending on window choice \cite{mi_exploring_2026}, reinforcing that the present sweep should be read as suggestive rather than as evidence of a universal optimum.

The influence of window size (ws), gap size (gs), and imminent-skip period (isp) on data preprocessing and segmentation is illustrated in three example-configurations: (i) default parameters (ws = 1s, gs = 2s, isp = 3s; Figure \ref{fig:labeling_strategy}), (ii) reduced window size (ws = 0.3s, gs = 2s, isp = 3s; Figure \ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_window}), and (iii) reduced gap with extended imminent-skip period (ws = 1s, gs = 0.5s, isp = 4s; Figure \ref{fig:labeling_strategy_small_gap_big_isp}). The swipe event is indicated by vertical red lines. All figures show the same randomly selected 70-second segment from a single participant. Time (seconds) is plotted on the x-axis, and class labels---STAY (blue) and SKIP (red)---on the y-axis.

