Cohen’s \(d\) is the standard scale-invariant index for quantifying the standardized difference between two distributions, expressing a mean difference in units of pooled standard deviation rather than in the arbitrary units of the underlying measure \cite{cohen_statistical_1988}. This property is what makes it suitable for comparing separability across heterogeneous feature types — spectral power, entropy, connectivity, or raw amplitude — that would otherwise be incommensurable on their native scales. The metric is established specifically in EEG-based group and class separability analysis: Henao Isaza et al.\ use Cohen’s \(d\) to rank EEG-derived spectral, connectivity, and entropy features by their ability to separate Alzheimer's-risk carriers from healthy controls, treating it as a principled feature-selection criterion \cite{henao_isaza_comprehensive_2026}. Kiarashi et al.\ apply the same metric to quantify group separability between MCI and cognitively normal participants directly from model output distributions, and further show that separability under Cohen’s \(d\) responds systematically to methodological choices — in their case, multi-night signal aggregation — with (d) increasing alongside statistical significance as aggregation improved \cite{kiarashi_sleep-derived_2025}. Taken together, this prior work establishes two points relevant to the present work: first, that Cohen’s \(d\) is an appropriate and validated tool for ranking discriminative EEG features independent of their native units, motivating its use here for feature-level class-separation ranking (Section~\ref{sec:results_feature_importance}); and second, that Cohen’s \(d\) is equally appropriate as a sensitivity metric for tracking how a labeling or preprocessing parameter choice affects downstream separability, motivating its use here for the labeling-parameter sensitivity analysis (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}). Neither prior use, however, involves a self-paced behavioral labeling scheme comparable to the STAY/SKIP paradigm applied here — extending this prior work to that specific setting is a contribution of the present work.

Cohen’s \(d\) expresses the standardized difference between the STAY and SKIP distributions, making it suitable for comparing separability across parameter settings even when the underlying distributions differ in scale. In this context, larger absolute values of Cohen’s \(d\) indicate stronger separation between the two classes.

\begin{equation}
    d = \frac{\bar{x}_{\text{STAY}} - \bar{x}_{\text{SKIP}}}{\sqrt{\frac{(n_{\text{STAY}} - 1)s_{\text{STAY}}^2 + (n_{\text{SKIP}} - 1)s_{\text{SKIP}}^2}{n_{\text{STAY}} + n_{\text{SKIP}} - 2}}}
    \label{eq:cohens_d}
\end{equation}
where \(\bar{x}_{\text{STAY}}\) and \(\bar{x}_{\text{SKIP}}\) denote the sample means of the feature values for the STAY and SKIP windows respectively, \(s_{\text{STAY}}^2\) and \(s_{\text{SKIP}}^2\) are the corresponding sample variances, and \(n_{\text{STAY}}\) and \(n_{\text{SKIP}}\) represent the number of samples in each class.

For each parameter configuration, Cohen’s \(d\) was computed independently between the STAY and SKIP distributions of every extracted univariate feature (e.g.\ \texttt{TP10\_raw\_std}), yielding one \(|d|\) value per feature. This provided a direct quantitative index of how strongly the chosen temporal definitions emphasized the contrast between the \texttt{STAY} and \texttt{SKIP} behavioral classes. The two levels at which \(|d|\) is reported in the feature-ranking analyses---exact per-feature values versus category means---are described in Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d}.

