\section{The Engagement Index's Swipe-Prediction Performance}
\label{sec:results_engagement_index}

\subsection{Feature Importance and Neurophysiological Correlates}
\label{sec:results_feature_importance}

In Figure~\ref{fig:cohend_by_freq_band}, we observe that class separation, quantified via Cohen’s \(d\) as described in Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_separation_cohen_d}, is consistently stronger for several individual frequency bands (blue bars) than for the conventional Engagement Index (red dotted line). In particular, the \(\alpha\)-band (8–13 Hz) achieves an effect size approximately 0.005 standard deviations higher than the Engagement Index, and the high-\(\gamma\) band (40–60 Hz), as the strongest candidate, exceeds it by more than 0.03 standard deviations. The Engagement Index — which combines three bands into a single scalar — itself reaches just above 0.02 and is therefore weaker in class separability than any of the individual frequency bands and the raw signal. This suggests that aggregating multiple bands into a single composite metric may dilute discriminative information that is more clearly expressed within specific, well-chosen frequency ranges.

Moreover, class separation reaches values above 0.04 only for a small subset of bands: the raw, broadband signal (without frequency decomposition), the \(\delta\) band (1–4 Hz), the low-\(\gamma\) band (30–40 Hz), and the high-\(\gamma\) band (40–60 Hz). These results indicate that both very low-frequency activity and specific \(\gamma\)-range oscillations carry comparatively strong discriminative structure for the SKIP versus STAY decision. The prominence of the \(\gamma\) range is consistent with gamma activity (30--100~Hz) being associated with high levels of cognitive processing, perception, and attention \cite{bellos_methods_2025}. This is consistent with evidence that delta oscillatory responses are involved in decision-making and attentional processes during cognitive tasks \cite{guntekin_review_2016}, and that delta activity is implicated in salience detection, becoming functionally more prominent when higher-frequency, more operationally advanced processing is less dominant \cite{knyazev_eeg_2012} — a pattern structurally consistent with the present finding that band-specific features outperform the composite, higher-frequency-weighted Engagement Index.

Taken together, these findings highlight three key points. First, individual frequency bands exhibit stronger class-separation capability than the conventional Engagement Index, suggesting that analyses based on raw or band-specific signals may be more informative than combined engagement metrics. Second, the raw broadband signal reaches a higher mean \(|d|\) than all separated bands except high \(\gamma\), consistent with prior EEG decoding work showing broadband/undecomposed signal can match or exceed band-limited features depending on task \cite{ovi_frequency_2026}. This comparison should be read with care, since the raw signal is summarized by only three time-domain statistics whereas each band is summarized by eight (Section~\ref{sec:method_feature_engineering}); it nevertheless motivates closer examination of both broadband information and high-\(\gamma\) activity in particular.

% CITE-NEEDED P20: "have been reported in EEG classification more broadly" | was: precedent | adjacent \cite{plucinska_impact_2022} | verify quote against source PDF
Third, the pattern across bands follows a clear up–down–up trend: separability is high at low frequencies, drops to a minimum in the \(\alpha\) range (8–13 Hz), then rises to a global maximum in the high-\(\gamma\) range before decreasing again for very high \(\gamma\) frequencies (60–100 Hz). Non-monotonic, band-specific separability profiles of this kind have been reported in EEG classification more broadly; band-wise performance in EEG-based person verification has been shown to peak in non-adjacent frequency ranges rather than scaling smoothly with frequency \cite{plucinska_impact_2022}. This systematic profile offers a frequency-resolved view of where discriminative information is concentrated in the EEG. The \(\alpha\) minimum within this profile is notable in itself: alpha activity is classically interpreted as cortical idling --- the synchronized rhythm of regions that are neither processing sensory input nor generating motor output, which desynchronizes once a region is recruited for active processing \cite{pfurtscheller_event-related_1996} --- and a rhythm indexing momentary passivity rather than content appraisal would be expected to separate the two classes poorly.

