\fbox{%
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{Definition (Engagement).} \textit{Engagement is the volitional, effortful allocation of a person's limited attentional capacity to a particular object, sustained over a declared interval against available alternatives, and ending in a disengagement event that is observable independently of how long the allocation lasted.}
\vspace{0.5em}
\end{minipage}}
\end{center}

Each clause does work. Each excludes something.

% READER-LOAD (x4 in this subsection): Constraint~1, ~4, ~1 and~2, ~3 and~5 refer to the five numbered findings in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_constraints}. Those are \paragraph headings with no \label, so they are not even hyperlinks -- the reader must scroll back and count. Worst instance in the thesis. FIX: name each constraint in three or four words at the point of use.
\textbf{\textit{Volitional, effortful}} excludes dissociative absorption and pure habit. A viewer who cannot later recall what they watched \cite{baughan_i_2022} was present, not engaged. This is the clause that time-in-app violates, because a clock counts the dissociated viewer and the attentive one identically (Constraint~1).

\textbf{\textit{Allocation of a person's limited attentional capacity}} makes engagement a resource that is spent rather than a preference that is held. It is therefore zero-sum at the moment of measurement: engagement with one object is simultaneous evidence of non-engagement with everything else then available. Liking something is not engaging with it; paying for it in attention is.

\textbf{\textit{To a particular object}} requires that engagement always be engagement \emph{with} something --- a message, a task, a person, a piece of work. ``An engaged user'' or ``an engaged employee'', stated without an object, is not yet a measurable claim.

\textbf{\textit{Sustained over a declared interval}} makes the timescale part of the measurement rather than a result of it (Constraint~4). Engagement measured over three seconds and over three weeks are different quantities, and a figure reported without its interval cannot be interpreted or compared.

\textbf{\textit{Against available alternatives}} distinguishes engagement from captivity. Attention that has nowhere else to go --- a mandatory meeting, a queue, a waiting room --- is occupancy, not engagement. This is also why an infinite feed is an unusually clean measurement setting: the alternative is always exactly one thumb movement away.

\textbf{\textit{Ending in a disengagement event observable independently of how long the allocation lasted}} is the clause that carries the measurement weight (Constraints~1 and~2). It asserts that the end of engagement is something that happens, not merely a level that drops, and it forbids the clock from serving as the evidence for it. The four-stage process model of O'Brien and Toms anticipated this by naming disengagement as a distinct stage, but reconstructed those stages from retrospective interviews and so could not supply a time-resolved criterion for when the stage begins \cite{obrien_what_2008}.

\paragraph{What it inherits.} From O'Brien and Toms, that engagement is a process with a beginning, a middle, and an end, and that disengagement is a stage of it rather than the absence of one \cite{obrien_what_2008}. From intervention science, that engagement is the precondition for effect and therefore worth measuring in its own right \cite{yardley_understanding_2016, perski_conceptualising_2017}. From attention research, that it is a fluctuating state rather than a stable trait \cite{debettencourt_sustained_2021}.

\paragraph{What it drops.} Duration, which the behavioural half of every current definition includes \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017, trunfio_conceptualising_2021}, is removed from the definition and demoted to an indicator. Every named instrument --- index, scale, rating, or telemetry --- is removed with it.

\paragraph{What it adds.} Three requirements no existing definition states: a declared timescale, without which the measured quantity is undefined; an exit criterion that must be observable without reference to the clock; and the separation of the construct from whatever instrument is used to detect it.

What the definition deliberately omits is as important as what it contains. It names no indicator, no modality, and no formula (Constraints~3 and~5). It is compatible with an EEG feature, a self-report item, a linguistic marker, or a behavioural trace, and commits to none of them --- which is precisely what makes it portable across the settings where different instruments are the only ones available.

\subsection{Implications for Measurement}
\label{sec:engagement_measurement}

Three consequences follow directly, and they are the practical content of the definition.

\textbf{Duration is demoted from criterion to indicator, and a weak one.} It may be reported; it may not be used as the standard against which other measures are validated. Where duration and an exit-sensitive measure disagree, the definition sides with the latter.

\textbf{The exit is the primary measurement target.} An instrument should be judged on how well it detects the transition out of engagement, not on how well it scores the uneventful middle. Judged on that criterion, the field's standard physiological instrument fails in this paradigm (Section~\ref{sec:results_model_performance}) --- which is a finding about the instrument, not about the construct.

\textbf{Indicators are plural and must be validated against one another.} Because the construct is defined without an indicator, EEG features, self-report, and behavioural or linguistic traces are all candidate indicators of the same thing, and none of them \emph{is} the thing. The work this creates is convergent validation, not the selection of a winner.

