Operationally, these collapse into a reporting requirement. A well-formed engagement measurement states four things: the \textbf{object} engaged with, the \textbf{interval} over which engagement is claimed, the \textbf{indicator} used, and the \textbf{exit criterion}, meaning what would have counted as disengagement. A study that cannot state the fourth of these has measured occupancy rather than engagement.

\subsection{The Importance of Field Measurement}
\label{sec:engagement_bridge}

Physiological measurement of engagement is precise and not deployable. The pipeline used here required a headset, per-participant baseline normalisation, millisecond behavioural synchronisation, a distraction-free room, and within-participant calibration without which performance fell to chance (Section~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). None of that survives contact with a classroom, a hospital, a workplace, or a population. The definition above was written so that this is a practical limitation rather than a conceptual one: because the construct is specified without naming an indicator, a cheaper indicator may index the same construct.

On that basis, and as a hypothesis for follow-up research rather than a finding of this thesis:

\begin{quote}
\emph{The construct defined in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_definition} may be approximable, in settings where physiological measurement is infeasible at scale, by lower-cost and field-deployable proxies --- repeated self-report instruments, and behavioural or linguistic traces in naturally occurring activity and communication --- provided that such proxies are validated against a criterion preserving the two properties the present results identify as definitional: sensitivity to the disengagement event, and independence from duration.}
\end{quote}

% READER-LOAD: "the third" = agreement on the exit specifically, from the three-item list above. Reader must re-count. FIX: name it.
This is a conjecture, and the present data cannot test it. This thesis recorded a single twenty-five-minute session of one solitary activity per participant. It contains no repeated self-report, no linguistic data, no second setting, and no downstream outcome against which any proxy could be scored. Demonstrating the bridge would require three things it does not have: a setting in which physiological and proxy measures are collected from the same people at the same time; a disengagement event in that setting as unambiguous as a swipe --- the hard part, since most settings have no thumb; and agreement between proxy and physiological criterion on the \emph{exit} specifically, not merely on the average level. Until the third is shown, a proxy measures the proxy. The value of stating the conjecture precisely is that it names the experiment that would refute it.

\subsection{Limits of the Proposed Definition}
\label{sec:engagement_falsification}

Three findings would break it, and each is reachable with existing methods.

If a person-invariant continuous indicator of engagement were demonstrated --- one transferring across individuals without calibration --- then keeping indicators out of the definition would be unnecessary caution, and a stronger, instrument-bearing definition would be preferable to this one.

If disengagement were shown to have no locatable onset --- to be a smooth decline whose apparent ``moment'' is an artefact of where an analyst places a threshold --- the exit clause would fall, and with it most of the definition's measurement value. The parameter-sensitivity result reported here (Section~\ref{sec:method_parameter_sensitivity}) is in fact the strongest available argument for that possibility, and is the reason the clause requires the event to be \emph{observable} rather than instantaneous.

If dissociated, non-volitional consumption were shown to produce the same downstream outcomes as effortful attention --- the same recall, the same behaviour change --- then the volitional and effortful clauses would buy nothing, and duration could be rehabilitated as a criterion.

\subsection{Scope of the Proposed Definition}
\label{sec:engagement_scope}

Nothing in the definition is specific to short-form video. TikTok served here as a field site with an unusually convenient property: a clean, frequent, voluntary exit event, a few hundred observable disengagements per person per half-hour, which is what makes the exit clause testable at all. The construct is intended for settings where exits are rarer, slower, and messier --- a learner abandoning a course, a patient dropping out of a digital behaviour-change intervention \cite{perski_conceptualising_2017}, a colleague withdrawing from a shared piece of work --- and where, correspondingly, the field's habit of substituting duration for engagement is hardest to detect and most expensive to get wrong.

Because none of the three additions mentions a screen, a swipe, or an electrode, the definition holds wherever a person can attend to something and leave it. Engagement with a health message in a feed and engagement with shared work in a distributed team are, on this account, the same construct measured under different exit conditions: in the feed the exit is a thumb movement and is logged; in remote collaborative work it is slower, quieter, and visible only in what someone stops contributing to. That is precisely where substituting duration --- hours logged, messages sent, sessions attended --- for engagement is hardest to catch, and where a definition that forbids the substitution earns the most.

The contribution offered here is therefore not the effect sizes that constrain the definition, which are modest and provisional, but the definition and its four reporting requirements, which are neither. They are stated once, here, so that later work can cite them rather than re-derive them.


\chapter{Conclusion and Future Research}
\label{chap:conclusion}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This thesis asked whether the moment at which a person decides to stop watching can be seen in the brain before the thumb moves, and whether the field's conventional composite marker of engagement, the Engagement Index \(\beta/(\alpha+\theta)\), is the right instrument for seeing it. Twenty-five participants browsed their own algorithmic feed, unconstrained and alone, while a four-channel consumer headband recorded continuously and every swipe was marked to the millisecond. From that recording, 236 univariate features per one-second window were derived and evaluated against a chance baseline and against the Engagement Index, under both within-participant and across-participant validation. The answers that follow are modest in magnitude and deliberately narrow in scope, but they are not ambiguous.

On separability, the answer is a qualified yes. The period preceding a voluntary skip is not statistically indistinguishable from continuous viewing: individual features separate the two classes reliably, and the strongest of them, raw-signal standard deviation at TP10 (\(|d| = 0.196\)), does so with roughly ten times the standardized effect of the Engagement Index. The magnitudes remain small in absolute terms, and no single feature approaches the separability that would be required for individual-level inference. What the data support is the existence of the signal, not its sufficiency.

% READER-LOAD: former/latter = continuing vs leaving, from the previous clause. Load is small but this is the Conclusion's key sentence. FIX: repeat the two words.
On the benchmark comparison, the answer is more pointed, and it is the central empirical contribution of this work. The Engagement Index is not uninformative; it is \emph{asymmetrically} informative. It detects sustained, non-skip viewing well --- reaching 89.2\% recall for \texttt{STAY} at the inter-subject level --- and detects imminent disengagement poorly. Three features identified through effect-size ranking (TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-gamma mean, AF8 delta peak frequency) each improved \texttt{SKIP} detection significantly over that baseline at both levels of analysis, yet in the cross-participant setting their absolute performance (F\(_1\)-test up to 45.5\%) remained below the chance-equivalent reference. The correct reading is therefore not that a better index has been found, but that the existing one has a specific and locatable blind spot: it tracks the state of continuing, not the transition to leaving. A metric that measures the former and is used to reason about the latter will systematically mistake the absence of a decision for the presence of engagement.

