On parameter sensitivity, the answer is a caution addressed to the method rather than to the phenomenon. Class separability rose monotonically as the labeled window was lengthened and as it was moved further from the swipe, and the proportion of swipes classified as isolated rather than bursty fell from 67.4\% to 48.9\% on the strength of a single threshold choice. Separability, in this paradigm, is not a property of the brain alone; it is a joint property of the brain and the labeling scheme imposed upon it. Any reported effect size here --- including every one above --- is conditional on three temporal parameters that, to our knowledge, no prior work had established for a self-paced behavioral event and were therefore fixed by argument rather than by evidence.

What this thesis cannot conclude deserves to be stated as plainly as what it can. The two features carrying the most discriminative weight are also the two most exposed to contamination: temporal-site broadband activity sits directly over the temporalis muscle, whose myogenic output is broadband, present at rest, and not fully removable by standard correction; and the high-gamma band straddles the 50~Hz powerline-noise on hardware known to introduce intermodulation artifacts around it. Neither confound was resolved here. Two physical devices were used across the cohort, so hardware effects cannot be separated from participant effects. The sample was demographically narrow and academically skewed. The reported \(p\)-values are uncorrected across a family of comparisons and should be read as screening criteria. It is entirely possible that some of what looks like a brain signal is really jaw muscle activity. Figuring out how much of the effect is real and how much is muscle artifact is the most important thing to do next — not a nice-to-have, but a requirement before these findings can be trusted.

What does survive these caveats is a paradigm and an instrument. The experiment demonstrates that ecologically valid short-form video consumption --- a personal feed, no imposed stimulus, no fixation, no trial structure --- can be recorded with millisecond behavioral synchronization on consumer hardware, and analyzed reproducibly end to end with open-source components. That means this kind of question no longer needs an expensive clinical lab: a laptop and a consumer headband are enough. And it gets there without needing any data from the platforms themselves.

One further conclusion is not about TikTok at all, and is stated in Section~\ref{sec:engagement_updated}: the results above constrain what engagement can be taken to mean as a general construct, and that chapter sets out a definition independent of any instrument, the four things any engagement measurement must therefore report, and an explicitly hypothetical bridge from physiological measurement to the cheaper proxies that field settings would require.

That last point is why this work matters beyond its effect sizes. Billions of these micro-decisions are made every day, and the systems that shape them are optimized against a proxy --- watch time --- that the literature reviewed here shows to be equally consistent with absorbed attention and with dissociated, unencoded consumption. This thesis provides no license to claim that the neural precursor of a swipe has been decoded. It provides something smaller and more durable: evidence that the moment of leaving has a physiological correlate distinct from the state of staying, that the field's standard engagement metric largely misses it, and that the tools required to study it are now within reach of the public institutions whose messages must survive that same feed. Attention is finite, unevenly distributed, and, for the moment, measured almost exclusively by those with an interest in consuming it. Building an independent, transparent, and falsifiable account of how it is lost is not a neuroscientific curiosity. It is a precondition for anyone else --- educators, clinicians, public health communicators --- to compete on honest terms for the seconds that decide \textit{what a population knows - and acts on.}

\section{Future Research}
\label{sec:future_research}

Future research should first focus on validation and replication. The present findings are promising, but they are based on a single dataset with a relatively small sample size and two physical units of the same recording device. Replication in an independent sample is therefore essential, ideally with more uniform hardware, tighter artifact control, a broader and less academically-skewed participant pool, and multiple-comparison-corrected significance testing across the feature--baseline comparisons reported here. A key priority should be to test whether the same features -- especially TP10 raw standard deviation, AF7 high-\(\gamma\) mean, and AF8 delta peak frequency -- remain informative under repeated acquisition, different content types, and different user populations.

A second major direction is mechanistic clarification. The present results leave open whether the strongest effects reflect genuinely neural engagement processes, movement-related artifacts, or a mixture of both. Future work should therefore include explicit artifact analyses, such as electromyographic control across the alpha-through-gamma range (not only high-gamma or broadband), motion monitoring, ICA-based comparison, channel-quality assessments, and device-specific sensitivity checks. It would also be valuable to compare broadband features against strictly cleaned, source-reconstructed, or artifact-masked data to determine how much of the predictive signal survives after conservative preprocessing.  
