Overall, the present study opens a broad but carefully bounded research program: one that combines explainable EEG analysis, naturalistic behavior, and applied communication research. Its value lies not in claiming a final solution, but in identifying a credible path toward more interpretable, behaviorally grounded, and potentially useful models of engagement and disengagement in real-world digital environments.

\chapter{Plain-Language Summary: Common Questions}
\label{chap:plain_language}

The questions that dominate public debate about short-form video are not the questions an EEG study is built to answer. They are, however, the questions on which people actually act --- as parents, as teachers, as voters, as regulators, and as communicators trying to reach an audience. This chapter therefore steps out of technical register and answers the most common of those questions in ordinary language, using only what this thesis and the literature it reviews can support. It deliberately revisits ground already covered in Chapters~\ref{chap:results} and~\ref{chap:conclusion}, restated for a reader who has not read them.

Three cautions apply to everything below. This was one study of 25 young adults, mostly students, each recorded once for 25 minutes. It measured brain activity in the seconds around a swipe; it did not measure anyone's health, mood, grades, sleep, or long-term wellbeing, and it followed nobody over time. Every answer here is therefore a \emph{direction of evidence}, not a settled finding, and is written to be revised. Where the honest answer is ``this study cannot tell you,'' that is stated plainly rather than filled in with plausible-sounding inference.

\paragraph{Is short-form video damaging my brain?}
This study cannot tell you, and nobody should cite it as evidence either way. It never looked for damage. It compared two ordinary moments --- still watching, versus about to swipe away --- in healthy young adults over 25 minutes. What it did find is that those two moments look measurably different in the brain, which says the decision to move on is a real physical event and not merely a habit or a figure of speech. That is a statement about how attention works, not about harm. Claims in circulation that scrolling ``rewires'' or ``shrinks'' the brain go well beyond what evidence of this kind can carry.
\emph{What to do with this:} treat confident claims in both directions with suspicion. The strong version of the harm story and the dismissive ``it's all moral panic'' reply are both running ahead of the data.

\paragraph{If I watch something for a long time, does that mean I was interested in it?}
Probably less often than you would assume, and this is the clearest practical message of the thesis. Platforms, advertisers and much public commentary treat time spent watching as if it measured interest. But long, uninterrupted viewing is just as consistent with being absorbed as with being zoned out and later remembering almost nothing \cite{baughan_i_2022, knierim_flow_2026}. The measurement side of this thesis points the same way: the standard laboratory formula for ``engagement'' (Section~\ref{sec:results_model_performance}) was far worse at telling these two moments apart than several plain, single measurements of the raw signal --- the best of which separated them by close to ten times as much. If watch time reliably captured engagement, that gap should not exist.
\emph{What to do with this:} stop reading watch time as proof that something worked. For anyone reporting reach --- health campaigns especially --- a high view count is a statement about distribution, not about whether anything landed. Ask separately whether people acted, returned, or could recall the message.

\paragraph{Can a cheap headband read my mind, or tell what I am about to do?}
No --- not in any sense that should worry you today, and the failures are as informative as the successes. Using a consumer headband costing a few hundred euros, the signal that distinguished ``about to swipe'' from ``still watching'' was real but weak, and it worked only when the system was tuned to one specific person. Trained on a group and applied to a new individual, performance collapsed to roughly guessing (Section~\ref{sec:results_alternative_index}). In plain terms: there is a faint, person-specific fingerprint, not a readable thought.
\emph{What to do with this:} do not buy claims that wearable brain sensors can currently detect engagement, attention or intent in a general population. Equally, note the direction of travel: this was four cheap sensors, one short session, one small group. Organisations with far more data and far longer observation are not bound by those limits, which is the real reason to ask now who is permitted to measure attention and under what oversight.

\paragraph{Why is it so hard to stop scrolling once I start?}
This study offers one small, concrete piece of the picture. Swipes do not arrive evenly spaced. They cluster into short bursts --- runs of rapid skipping with calmer stretches in between (Section~\ref{sec:results_burst_analysis}). About two-thirds of swipes were isolated, so most of the time people were not in a burst at all. But how burst-prone someone was varied enormously between individuals, far more than any average conveys. The pattern is real; the ``it'' that happens is not the same for everyone.
\emph{What to do with this:} if you want to interrupt your own scrolling, the useful target is the fast run rather than the total minutes, since the two are not the same behaviour. And be sceptical of advice derived from an average user, because on this measure the average user barely exists.

\paragraph{Should phones be banned in schools, or social media restricted by age?}
This thesis has nothing to say about either policy, and it would be misleading to enlist it on either side. It tested no intervention, involved no minors, and measured no outcome that such a policy would target. One indirect observation is worth carrying into that debate, however: the behaviour measured here differed so much from person to person that a single rule calibrated to the average would fit very few individuals well. That is a caution about uniform rules generally, not an argument for or against any particular one.
\emph{What to do with this:} demand that whoever proposes a rule states which outcome it is supposed to improve and how that will be checked afterwards. This study cannot supply that evidence, and neither can most of what is currently cited in the debate.

\paragraph{What should regulators actually require, if not bans?}
The most defensible ask suggested by this work is disclosure rather than prohibition. Feeds are tuned to maximise something, and the quantity most commonly optimised --- continued watching --- is, on the evidence reviewed here, not a reliable indicator that the viewer is attending, benefiting, or even remembering. Optimising it is therefore not the same as serving the user, and the gap between the two is currently invisible from the outside. A further practical point: this study was run on off-the-shelf hardware by a single researcher, which means independent measurement of these questions is now cheap enough that no regulator need rely solely on platform-supplied figures.
\emph{What to do with this:} require platforms to disclose what their systems optimise for and to open those targets to outside audit; fund small independent replication rather than waiting for internal data to be volunteered.

\paragraph{I make health or education content. How do I hold attention?}
Honestly: this thesis does not yet give you a tool. It shows that the moment of leaving has a physical signature, which is a precondition for one day detecting disengagement as it begins --- but the signal is currently too weak, and too person-specific, to build anything you could deploy. What it does give you is a warning about your own dashboard. If you are judging content by average watch time, you are optimising against a number that cannot distinguish an absorbed viewer from a dissociated one, and you may be steadily selecting for content that holds eyes without landing a message.
\emph{What to do with this:} keep watch time as a distribution check, but judge success on something downstream that you actually care about --- recall, clicks, appointments, behaviour change --- and accept that this is harder and slower to measure. That difficulty is not a reason to fall back on the easier number.

\paragraph{So what is the single most useful thing to take away?}
That the industry-standard way of measuring engagement is weaker than its ubiquity implies, and that better measurement is now within reach of people outside the platforms. This thesis does not show that anyone has decoded the decision to swipe; it shows that the moment of leaving is physically distinguishable from the state of staying, that the conventional engagement metric largely misses it, and that the equipment required to investigate this costs a few hundred euros rather than a laboratory budget. Whether that capability is used to make feeds more compelling or to hold them accountable is not a scientific question, and will not be settled by studies like this one.

